BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Zaiba Baig: Kainchee Lagaa & Jhooti
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260616T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260616T200000
UID:69f3a6cbcdd2f7e31fe3c274@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Zaiba Baig (photo credit: Maxholio)\nJoin playwright and autho
 r Zaiba Baig for a conversation about\nKainchee Lagaa & Jhooti: The Beggin
 g Brown Bitch Plays\n\, two daring and subversive dramas that challenge th
 e white savior complex and blur the line between truth and performance thr
 ough unreliable narrators.\nIn Kainchee Lagaa\, two estranged siblings rec
 onnect across space and time as painful memories and family trauma resurfa
 ce. In\nJhooti\n\, a trans woman on the run\, carrying a plastic bag full
  of her belongings\, pleads for protection. But soon her story unravels in
 to a dangerous web of destruction. Both plays plunge into the multidimensi
 onal depths of Brown trans women teetering on a tightrope between truth an
 d survival.\nBaig will discuss the themes\, characters and creative proces
 s behind these explosive works\, and how the plays use theatrical disrupti
 on to confront audiences and challenge dominant narratives.\nABOUT THIS EV
 ENT'S GUESTS:\nZaiba Baig\n(she/they) is a writer\, actor\, and producer f
 or stage and screen\, as well as a workshop developer and facilitator for
  art-focused non-profits. Baig’s published works include their first pla
 y\,\nAcha Bacha\n(2020\, nominated for the Dayne Ogilvie prize from the Wr
 iter’s Trust of Canada)\, and an anthology of monologues they co-edited 
 for queer/trans artists titled\nThis Is Beyond\n(2023). Baig was a co-crea
 tor\, executive producer\, and lead actor in their Peabody Award–winning
  series\nSort Of\n(2021–2023) for which Baig received the Canadian Scree
 n Award for comedy writing and performance\, as well as a Gotham Award nom
 ination for performance in a new series. Baig has facilitated creative wri
 ting and playwriting programs for trans artists\, Black\, Indigenous\, and
  People of Colour (BIPOC) artists\, and trans youth including\, Trans Gem
 mes (2019 2020)\, Paprika Festival’s Playwrights Unit (2019–2023)\, Be
 ing Me (2023–)\, and Dialogue Dolls (2023).\nNedra Rodrigo\n(she/they) 
 is the founder of the Tamil Studies Symposium at York University and the q
 ueer-positive bilingual BlPOC event series\, The Tam Fam Lit Jam. She is 
 a translator\, academic\, curator of multi-arts events and advisor for the
  Tamil Community Centre Project in Scarborough. Nedra’s published transl
 ations include\nIn the Shadow of the Sword\nand\nthe\nDevakanthan quintet\
 nPrison of Dreams\nfor which she received the Tamil Literary Garden’s Tr
 anslation Award for 2024. Her essays and translations have appeared in sev
 eral prominent journals and anthologies\n.\nShe co-edited the anthology\nT
 amil Terrains\nwith Geetha Sukumaran. She is a recipient of the PEN Transl
 ates SALT Award for 2025 for her translations of Rashmy’s collection of 
 poems\nSongs in a Time of Confinement\n. She has conducted workshops in co
 mmunity capacity building\, literature\, archives\, and translation in sch
 ools\, universities and community.\n**\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation\n: Beet
 on Hall\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 1st floor\nTime:\
 nBook signing starts at 6:00pm. Program starts at 7:00pm.\nTickets\n: Free
  registration required.\nTicket policy\n: We oversell these events to make
  sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Ti
 ckets are guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Any remain
 ing space will be opened up to the rush line beginning at 6:45pm. Seating 
 first come\, first served. Tickets do not guarantee seats.\n**\nThis event
  is a part of spotlight Pride programming. Visit\ntpl.ca/pride\nfor more l
 istings.\n**\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on 
 this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libr
 aries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only 
 be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation.\nReg
 istrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program
 \n. Questions about how the library handles your information can be direct
 ed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Str
 eet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall (1st Floor)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f3a6cbcdd2f7e31fe3c274
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
