BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google AI Essentials: Online Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T200000
UID:69fb97a3e7108c0f03d128e4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about artificial intelligence or want to learn practic
 al AI skills for work and personal use? Join our staff-facilitated learnin
 g circle to complete the self-paced Google AI Essentials specialization.\n
 This 10-hour introductory-level specialization\, offered through Coursera\
 , is available for free to learning circle participants. It is made up of 
 five courses\, each containing four units that consist of short videos\, a
 rticles\, assignments and a short multiple-choice quiz.\nBy completing the
  specialization\,\nyou will learn:\nHow AI works\nHow to write effective p
 rompts\nHow to use generative AI tools responsibly\nHow to identify AI's p
 otential biases and avoid harm\nHow to stay up to date with AI news\nAfter
  you complete the course\, you earn a Google certificate and a badge to sh
 are with others\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary staf
 f for people who want to complete the Google AI Essentials specialization 
 together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work together thr
 ough course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to attending a
 ll four learning circle sessions.\nNo AI experience is required.\nHow to J
 oi\nn a Learning Circle:\nRegister once on the first session of this event
  series\, in order to attend all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid e
 mail address\n.\nA valid TPL library card is required for online circles
 .\nAttend all four sessions (online via Zoom\, date: June 4-June 25\, 2026
 )\nRegistration is a two-step procedure. First\, participants must complet
 e their registration through this page. After this step\, library staff wi
 ll follow up to request additional information in order to complete the re
 gistration.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on t
 his form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libra
 ries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only b
 e used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Quest
 ions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Ans
 werline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, 
 Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69fb97a3e7108c0f03d128e4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ericka Hart\, Nasty Work
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T203000
UID:6a031ed38e677a28001b7031@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Ericka Hart\nAn award-winning sexuality educator takes down so
 ciety’s deeply entrenched colonial views on sex and gender throughout hi
 story in this accessible\, candid\, and revolutionary exploration of how w
 e can—and should—reclaim our minds and bodies for a more pleasurable e
 xistence for all.\nWhen you think about sex ed\, your mind likely goes bac
 k to those uncomfortable school desks and the stifled laughs of your teena
 ge years. But what we’ve been socialized to believe about sexuality actu
 ally hinders our own pleasure well into adulthood. Whether we know it or n
 ot\, even the most progressive among us are often using 400-year-old inher
 ited thoughts and belief systems in the twenty-first century. Why are we s
 till carrying forth these ancient values that have never served the vast m
 ajority?\nAs a Black\, queer\, non-binary\, disabled femme\, Ericka Hart b
 elieves that sex ed done right can actually be a tool for liberation. In\n
 Nasty Work\n\,\nshe breaks down the ways that social implications keep us 
 from experiencing pleasure\, particularly for marginalized communities acr
 oss race\, gender\, sexuality\, and ability\, and how we can dismantle the
 se oppressive myths. From examining what guides our attraction to others t
 o the history of consent\, Ericka Hart takes the blinders off and reveals 
 a more empowering view of sex and sexuality.\nNasty Work\nblends eye-openi
 ng research with powerful\, poignant personal narrative that disrupts ever
 ything you thought you knew about sex and society\, offering a liberatory 
 framework that makes pleasure accessible for all.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUES
 TS:\nEricka Hart\n\, M.Ed. (she/they) is a Black\, queer\, non-binary\, f
 emme\, activist\, writer and award-winning sexuality educator with a Maste
 r’s of Education in Human Sexuality from Widener University. Ericka’s 
 work broke ground when she went topless showing her double mastectomy scar
 s in public in 2016. Since then\, she has been in demand at colleges and u
 niversities across the country\, featured in countless digital and print p
 ublications like Vogue\, Washington Post\, Allure\, Harper’s Bazaar\, VI
 CE\, PAPER Mag\, BBC News\, Cosmopolitan\, Vanity Fair\, W Magazine\, Glam
 our\, Elle\, and Essence. Ericka’s voice is rooted in leading edge thoug
 ht around human sexual expression as inextricable to overall human health 
 and its intersections with race\, gender\, chronic illness and disability.
  Both radical and relatable\, she continues to push well beyond the thresh
 old of sex positivity. For the past 15 years\, she has taught comprehensiv
 e\, trauma-informed\, consent- and pleasure-based sex education from an an
 ti-racist\, queer lens at the elementary\, high school\, undergraduate and
  graduate levels. This includes 4 years at Columbia University’s School 
 of Social Work\, the CUNY School of Public Health at Hunter College and no
 w as the founding instructor of their own AASECT certified sexuality educa
 tion training program\, S\nex Ed as Resistance\,\nwhich has matriculated m
 ore than 250 students as of today. More significantly\, though\, Ericka is
  a bratty\, switchy\, Sagittarius\, service bottom and misses Whitney more
  than you.\nProfessor SA Smythe\n(they/them) is a critical theorist\, tra
 nsmedia storyteller\, and educator committed to black belonging beyond gen
 res and geographies. They are associate professor of Black Studies & the A
 rchive and Director of the Collaboratory for Black Poiēsis at the Univers
 ity of Toronto. Smythe is a Senior Fellow at the Center for Applied Transg
 ender Studies and serves on international editorial and advisory boards pr
 imarily dedicated to Black arts & culture\, trans & nonbinary liberation\,
  Black studies\, and human rights/migrant justice. Smythe is the editor of
 \nTroubling the Grounds: Global Configurations of Blackness\, Nativism\, a
 nd Indigeneity (vols. 1&2)\n(\nPostmodern Culture\n)\,\nTransnational Blac
 k Studies\n(Liverpool University Press)\, and author of\nWhere Blackness M
 eets the Sea: On Crisis\, Culture\, and the Black Mediterranean\n(forthcom
 ing) and\n[proclivity]\n\, a poetry collection and sound installation-perf
 ormance suite. Shortlisted for the 2025 Creative Capital Award (in Multime
 dia Performance)\, Smythe is recipient of the 2021-22 Rome Prize for Moder
 n Italian Studies and numerous composer/artist fellowships and residencies
  supporting their transmedia artwork including 2025 Leighton Studios at Ba
 nff Centre for Arts & Creativity and 2023-24 MacDowell Fellowship in Multi
 media Installation. For decades\, Smythe has organized with literary/perfo
 rmance\, abolitionist\, and migrant support collectives across Turtle Isla
 nd\, Europe\, and the Mediterranean.\n**\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBra
 m and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, S
 econd floor\nTime:\nDoors open at 6:00 pm\, program runs from 6:30 - 7:30
  pm and book signing starts at 7:40 pm until 8:20 pm.\nTickets:\nFree reg
 istration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to make sure
  that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets
  are guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Any remaining s
 pace will be opened up to the rush line beginning at 6:45pm. Seating first
  come\, first served. Tickets do not guarantee seats.\n**\nThis event is a
  part of spotlight Pride programming. Visit\ntpl.ca/pride\nfor more listin
 gs.\n**\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this 
 form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries
  Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be us
 ed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions
  about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerl
 ine staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toro
 nto ON M4W 2G8.\n**\nAccessibili\nty at this program:\nThis program will b
 e presented with ASL interpretation. Additional supports are outlined belo
 w.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Second F
 loor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a031ed38e677a28001b7031
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Sinye Tang
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T235959
UID:6a299f022ea730c17ab5e098@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of June\, works by artist Sinye Tang are 
 on display at Don Mills Branch.\nSinye Tang (she/her) is a Chinese-Canadia
 n artist\, food enthusiast\, and amateur kayaker residing in Toronto. Her 
 artwork is informed by urban forays into nature and in search of deliciou
 s bites. She enjoys starting her watercolours in nature or on the water\, 
 and collaborates often with her husband\, Evan Manuella\, on line and was
 h.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogram. Toronto Pu
 blic Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at loc
 ations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural
  interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed 
 during branch open hours.\nFree. All are welcome.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Main Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a299f022ea730c17ab5e098
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
