BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Modern Fairy Tales on Film: Ex Machina
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260626T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260626T130000
UID:6a207e7c8ea300e2631adb54@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this final instalment of Toronto Public Library's virtual s
 eries on Modern Fairy Tales on Film\, host Adam Nayman discusses\nEx Machi
 na\n\, Alex Garland's prescient 2014 science fiction film. A searching exp
 loration of artificial intelligence\, as well as a scathing critique of la
 te-capitalistic corporatism\, this techno-thriller is a rare instance of s
 tunning visual effects being put in service of intelligently handled theme
 s. With the rise of AI\,\nEx Machina\nonly grows in relevance\, and in thi
 s conversation\, host Adam Nayman\, novelist Iain Reid\, and critic Katie 
 Kadue unpack its increasingly urgent themes\, as well as the archetypal qu
 alities that give it a timeless air.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nIain Rei
 d\nhas written five books\, including the\nNew York Times\nbestselling nov
 el\nI’m Thinking of Ending Things\n\, which has been translated into mor
 e than twenty languages and was adapted into a Netflix film by Oscar winne
 r Charlie Kaufman. His second novel\,\nFoe\n\, was also adapted to film\, 
 starring Saoirse Ronan and Paul Mescal. His third novel\,\nWe Spread\n\, w
 as nominated for the Governor General’s Literary Award.\nKatie Kadue\nis
  an assistant professor in the Department of English at SUNY Binghampton. 
 Her writing has appeared in the academic journals\nModern Philology\n\,\nM
 ontaigne Studies\nand\nStudies in Philology\n\, as well as in venues such 
 as\nn+1\n\, the\nLos Angeles Review of Books\n\, and the\nChronicle of Hig
 her Education.\nAdam Nayman\nis a critic\, teacher\, and author\, whose bo
 oks include critical surveys of the Coen Brothers\, Paul Thomas Anderson\,
  and most recently\, in\nMind Games\n\, the filmmaker David Fincher. He is
  a regular contributor to\nThe Ringer\n\, a lecturer at the University of 
 Toronto's Cinema Studies Institute\, and he has written on film for\nThe N
 ew Yorker\nand\nThe New York Times\n.\n***\nImportant information for acce
 ssing this live virtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Ev
 entbrite to gain access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a 
 Zoom Webinar. Once registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both
  Zoom and Eventbrite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoo
 m Webinar link to watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must b
 e signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite with the same email address that you use
 d to book your ticket in order to access your unique event link. Unique ev
 ent links can only be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at
  Toronto Public Library\nWe are committed to making our content accessible
  to as many Torontonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are D
 eaf or have a disability and would like to request accommodation to partic
 ipate in this program. Please let us know as far in advance as possible an
 d we will do our best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice i
 s preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a207e7c8ea300e2631adb54
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
